00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:12.210
I wanted to get out of there,

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:16.880
but I was never, ever, ever
happy in my life, ever.

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:19.253
Even when I had everything
that you could want,

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:21.880
I was never a happy person.

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:23.940
But when I went to the center
and I realized, you know what,

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:25.480
here's a chance to actually,

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:27.640
I mean, I'm here, I'm not going anywhere,

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:29.200
they're not letting me out,

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:32.040
and I have a chance to
actually work on some things

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:33.910
and start digging into some things

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:35.050
and start looking at some things,

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:38.460
because there's nobody left to blame.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:40.690
I'm sitting here in a cell by myself.

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:42.590
There's nobody left to blame it on.

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:45.610
So, once they saw that I
was willing to do the work

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:47.700
and I really wanted to make
some changes in my life,

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:49.840
they went all out for me.

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:53.170
They are, the people at the
Florida Civil Commitment Center,

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:56.490
the clinical staff are amazing people.

00:11:56.490 --> 00:12:00.070
They, one example, there's a doctor there.

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:00.903
I hadn't cried.

00:12:00.903 --> 00:12:02.880
When I was a kid, my parents would beat us

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:04.260
and to stop us from crying,

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:06.103
there's actually a physiological,

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:10.490
you can swallow and stop
yourself from crying.

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:12.630
If you're ever crying,
swallow, you'll stop.

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:14.010
And my parents used to beat it into us,

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:15.163
swallow it, swallow it.

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:21.890
And so, as a result, I became
absolutely unable to cry.

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:23.900
I could not cry.

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:25.720
Well, one of the doctors in Arcadia

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:28.650
would literally take me
personally to her office,

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:29.800
one night, every week

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:32.010
and work on trying just to get me to cry.

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:33.560
And it took a long, long time.

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:34.850
And when we finally had a breakthrough

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:37.863
and I finally started
crying, it was an ugly,

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:39.830
ugly, ugly, cry.

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:42.280
But she did that on her own.

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:43.670
That wasn't part of her curriculum.

00:12:43.670 --> 00:12:46.120
That wasn't part of her job.

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:48.530
That wasn't part, she just did
that because she saw a need

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:50.372
that would benefit me.

00:12:50.372 --> 00:12:53.360
A way to express myself, a
way to release my emotions,

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:56.700
a way to handle that, without
having to stuff it all in.

00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:59.760
'Cause you just, nothing's big
enough to handle all of it.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:01.190
If you keep stuffing things in,

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:02.980
eventually it's gonna blow up.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:03.813
- [Interviewer] Would
you consider yourself

00:13:03.813 --> 00:13:07.183
one of the very few that's
broken out of this behavior?

00:13:07.183 --> 00:13:08.323
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:10.355
- [Interviewer] It's not
very common, I assume.

00:13:10.355 --> 00:13:13.110
- Not from a psychopathy, no.

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:17.003
The sexual desires, it's
like any other addiction.

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:21.890
It's, there's a lot of
people that have overcome it,

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:23.510
that don't wanna be sex offenders.

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:24.960
I never met one sex offender

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.100
and I've been doing this for 30 years.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:28.470
I never met one sex offender

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:30.380
that wanted to be a sex offender.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:32.140
Never met one sex offender that was happy

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:32.973
with what they were doing,

00:13:32.973 --> 00:13:34.190
that thought what they
were doing was right.

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:36.090
I mean, everybody knows it's wrong.

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:38.640
It was just, we couldn't stop ourselves.

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:39.900
Because it's an addiction.

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:41.210
And just like any other addiction,

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:43.430
you're gonna put yourself
in temptation's way

00:13:43.430 --> 00:13:45.230
and you're going to fold eventually.

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:49.740
But as far as the whole
learning how to think and feel

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:51.560
and all that, yeah. I don't
think there's a lot of people

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:54.275
that come back from that, I think-

00:13:54.275 --> 00:13:57.630
- [Interviewer] What was
the hardest part for you?

00:13:57.630 --> 00:13:59.960
- Accepting myself the way I am.

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:02.560
Accepting myself as imperfect.

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:06.145
'Cause I grew up in a household
where you had to be perfect.

00:14:06.145 --> 00:14:08.330
There was no room for error.

00:14:08.330 --> 00:14:10.723
There was, you didn't
bring home any but an A.

00:14:12.363 --> 00:14:16.800
You just had to, everything
had to be just perfect.

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:21.010
And so accepting myself as
imperfect and having problems

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:24.007
and being able to accept it.

00:14:24.007 --> 00:14:25.800
"Hey, here's something that
needs to be taken care of.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:27.740
Here's something that needs
to be looked at or addressed."

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:29.200
That was really difficult

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:30.930
'cause I just wanted to put my blinders on

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:33.600
and just go through life,
I'm good, everything's good.

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:34.534
- [Interviewer] Were drugs
or alcohol ever a part

00:14:34.534 --> 00:14:35.367
of your life?

00:14:35.367 --> 00:14:36.200
- Absolutely

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:37.842
Not alcohol.

00:14:37.842 --> 00:14:39.300
I mean, that's only a tolerance thing

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:40.540
just because I can't drink.

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:42.490
I just don't have the tolerance for it.

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:45.010
But drugs, yeah, absolutely.

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:47.180
- [Interviewer] And did that
play a part in the instances

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:48.530
that you-

00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:49.640
- Absolutely.

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:50.473
Absolutely.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:58.170
- [Interviewer] And what advice
can you give to anyone else

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:00.320
who's struggling with
something, thoughts like this

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:02.020
or desires like this?

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:04.350
- I would say talk to somebody.

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:09.313
Understand that it doesn't
have to be the way it is.

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:12.470
You can talk to somebody
and you can figure out,

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:14.420
there's something behind
what you're feeling.

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:16.630
There's something behind
what you're desiring.

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:20.760
And you're not gonna find it
acting out on those desires.

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:21.790
That's just a-

00:15:21.790 --> 00:15:22.660
- [Interviewer] There's
another level to it.

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:24.900
- Yeah, it's just a band-aid
that you're gonna put on it.

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:26.650
Behind it all, there's
something that you're missing.

00:15:26.650 --> 00:15:28.410
There's something that you need.

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:30.520
And talk to somebody

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:32.950
that can help you find out what that is.

00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:33.955
I think a lot of,

00:15:33.955 --> 00:15:38.600
a huge part of the whole sex
offending cycle is the secrecy.

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:40.440
It's such a burden to carry around.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:43.330
It's such a load that you
have to carry with you

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:45.660
that you're gonna fold underneath it.

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:48.160
And if you could find someplace that,

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:50.080
where you could talk to somebody,

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:51.860
and let all that out,

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:53.543
and not carry it around with you.

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:58.000
Sadly our system isn't set up for that.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.260
There's very few places in
this country where you can go

00:16:00.260 --> 00:16:02.090
and say, "Look this is what I'm feeling,"

00:16:02.090 --> 00:16:05.033
and not get into some problems.

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:08.430
But find somebody that you can talk to.

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:11.333
Before you act out, before
you act on the issues.

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:15.740
And unload it, don't
carry it around with you,

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:18.020
'cause it's not gonna go anywhere.

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:21.430
All it takes is the introduction
to some rational thought

00:16:21.430 --> 00:16:23.250
and to understand how to handle it.

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:27.740
It's not really that difficult to handle

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:29.060
once you're willing to do it,

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:31.160
once you're willing to be honest about it.

00:16:32.395 --> 00:16:33.243
But it's funny,

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:37.070
you hear society talk
about the sex offenders,

00:16:37.070 --> 00:16:39.400
the boogeyman, the child molester,

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:42.830
the bush jumpers, whatever.

00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:45.150
And yet nothing that they ever say or do

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:48.513
is nearly as harsh as what
you judge yourself as.

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:52.533
Nobody ever judges you as
harshly as you do yourself.

00:16:54.246 --> 00:16:57.727
And it's kind of hard to face the fact,

00:16:57.727 --> 00:17:01.310
"Hey, look I'm a sex
offender, I molested a child."

00:17:01.310 --> 00:17:04.983
It's a really hard, hard
portrait of yourself to look at.

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:10.110
Until you get to that point
where you can look at it

00:17:10.110 --> 00:17:13.430
and objectively and honestly address it,

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:16.060
you're just gonna be spinning your wheels.

00:17:16.060 --> 00:17:17.500
But once you get to that point,

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:19.547
once you really get to
a point where you're,

00:17:19.547 --> 00:17:21.470
"I don't wanna do this.

00:17:21.470 --> 00:17:23.307
This isn't right, I can't do this."

00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:26.920
You can figure your way through it.

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:28.520
But you gotta get to that point.

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:31.153
- [Interviewer] Excellent.

00:17:31.153 --> 00:17:32.900
Well, Bill, thank you so
much for talking with me.

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:33.733
- My pleasure.

00:17:33.733 --> 00:17:35.730
- [Interviewer] I'm
happy, I'm happy for you.

00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:36.563
- 30 years.

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:39.747
- [Interviewer] That's great, thank you.

